{"id":"SEC-001","category":"security","criterion":"input_validation","severity":"CRITICAL","score":1,"file":"head/.claude/hooks/safety/block-destructive-bash.sh","line":807,"code_snippet":"_seg_find_root=\"${BASH_REMATCH[3]}\"","reasoning_trace":"Compared base and head _re_find_exec_prefix. Head captures only the first whitespace-free token after `find` as group 3 and swallows everything up to -exec in an unchecked tail; the base loop that counted leading non-option operands and cleared the root unless exactly one was present is deleted. Ran both hook copies on multi-root shapes: head returns ALLOW (exit 0) for a find whose first root is ./build and second root is the home directory or /home with a placeholder-only recursive rm; base returned FR-2-AMBIGUOUS. Line 875 is the allow branch reached by the first root alone.","finding":"Multi-root find-exec recursive delete bypasses the destructive-command hook when only the first root is on the allow list.","critique":"A safety hook that classifies a command by one of its several roots is a protection-mechanism failure; the second root is never examined.","remediation":"Restore the exactly-one-root invariant: tokenize the span between the first root and -exec and blank _seg_find_root when another non-option operand appears, or widen the capture back to the full span and keep the count loop. Add hook fixtures for `find A B -exec rm -rf {} +`, `find A / -exec ...`, `find A ~ -exec ...` expecting BLOCK or AMBIGUOUS.","confidence":"high","references":["CWE-693 https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/693.html","CWE-184 https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/184.html","OWASP A04:2021 https://owasp.org/Top10/A04_2021-Insecure_Design/"]}
{"id":"SEC-002","category":"security","criterion":"availability","severity":"HIGH","score":2,"file":"head/.claude/adapters/loa_cheval/providers/anthropic_adapter.py","line":139,"code_snippet":"raise InvalidInputError(\n    f\"Anthropic API error (HTTP {status}): {_extract_error_message(err_json)}\"\n)","reasoning_trace":"Diff deletes _BILLING_CLASS_TOKENS and _is_billing_class_error and the two branches that raised ProviderUnavailableError for billing-class 4xx (streaming line 139-141, non-streaming line 223). retry.py handles ProviderUnavailableError by breaking to the next chain entry (line 422) and has no InvalidInputError arm; the ChevalError arm re-raises (lines 497-500), so the error is terminal. pytest on the existing tests/test_anthropic_adapter_billing_class.py against head: 9 failed, 3 passed, ImportError on the deleted helper. The test module's own docstring records the production symptom this classification fixed: every Flatline voice aborting on a depleted key although claude-headless would succeed.","finding":"Billing-class HTTP 4xx (credit balance, quota, invoice overdue) is now terminal InvalidInputError in both transport paths, so the cheval chain never falls back; the existing regression tests for this behavior fail.","critique":"Availability of the review, audit and Flatline gates depends on chain-walk when one account is billing-degraded, which this repository's own config says is the current state of the API path.","remediation":"Restore the billing-class branch in _complete_streaming and _complete_nonstreaming (or move the helper to base.py and share it across adapters). Make tests/test_anthropic_adapter_billing_class.py pass again.","confidence":"high","references":["CWE-755 https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/755.html","CWE-703 https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/703.html"]}
{"id":"SEC-003","category":"security","criterion":"authorization","severity":"HIGH","score":2,"file":"head/.claude/scripts/workflow-state.sh","line":83,"code_snippet":"sprint_dirs=$(find \"${a2a_dir}\" -maxdepth 1 -type d -name \"sprint-*\" 2>/dev/null || true)","reasoning_trace":"Base counted COMPLETED markers only for sprint-1..sprint-$total where total comes from sprint.md. Head counts every a2a/sprint-* directory holding a COMPLETED marker. determine_state at line 148 compares completed >= total and returns complete, which maps to the /deploy-production suggestion; lines 155 and 373 still iterate 1..total, so the counter and the state machine now disagree. Reproduced with a 2-sprint sprint.md and stale sprint-3..5/COMPLETED: base completed=0, head completed=3.","finding":"Stale or foreign sprint-* directories inflate the completed count, so the workflow reports complete and suggests production deployment while the current plan's sprints are unimplemented, unreviewed and unaudited.","critique":"The state detector is a gate on the implement -> review -> audit -> deploy path; letting history from earlier cycles satisfy it bypasses the gates.","remediation":"Restore the bounded 1..total loop, or intersect the found directories with ^sprint-[0-9]+$ and i <= total before counting. If a historical total is wanted, expose it as a separate field and never feed it into the completed >= total comparison. Add a test with stale COMPLETED directories beyond the current plan.","confidence":"high","references":["CWE-693 https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/693.html","CWE-841 https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/841.html"]}
{"id":"CQ-001","category":"code_quality","criterion":"error_handling","severity":"MEDIUM","score":2,"file":"head/.claude/scripts/workflow-state.sh","line":85,"code_snippet":"for dir in ${sprint_dirs}; do","reasoning_trace":"The newline-joined find output is expanded unquoted, so it is word-split on spaces and glob-expanded. Reproduced with a grimoire path containing a space and one COMPLETED marker: head returned completed=0.","finding":"Unquoted expansion of find output undercounts completed sprints when the project path contains whitespace, and re-globs on names containing glob characters.","critique":"The script runs under set -euo pipefail but the loop defeats that discipline; the bounded loop it replaced had no such hazard.","remediation":"Use find -print0 with `while IFS= read -r -d ''`, or a quoted glob loop, or restore the bounded loop.","confidence":"high","references":["CWE-20 https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html"]}
{"id":"CQ-002","category":"code_quality","criterion":"test_coverage","severity":"MEDIUM","score":1,"file":"head/.claude/hooks/safety/block-destructive-bash.sh","line":776,"code_snippet":"_re_find_exec_prefix='...find[[:space:]]+([^;&|)[:space:]]+)[^;&|)]*-exec$'","reasoning_trace":"The PR changes a safety-hook regex, an exception classification and a state counter, adds no test, and leaves tests/test_anthropic_adapter_billing_class.py red (9 failures). No fixture file for block-destructive-bash.sh exists in this snapshot; .claude/scripts/tests/test_dcg.sh exercises a different script and passes 37/37 unaffected. The implementation report contains no test evidence or AC Verification section.","finding":"Three behavior changes ship with no new or updated tests and an existing test module failing.","critique":"Each regression in this audit is the kind of case a fixture catches; the one fixture that existed now fails and the PR does not acknowledge it.","remediation":"Add hook fixtures for the multi-root and flags-before-root find-exec shapes, a workflow-state test with stale COMPLETED directories, and make the billing-class pytest module pass. Report the commands run in the implementation report.","confidence":"high","references":["CWE-1059 https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/1059.html"]}
{"id":"CQ-003","category":"code_quality","criterion":"documentation","severity":"LOW","score":2,"file":"head/.claude/hooks/safety/block-destructive-bash.sh","line":773,"code_snippet":"# is the FIND ROOT. Scope: the root token is `find`'s FIRST argument —\n# `find -L root -exec ...` (flags before root) is not covered and keeps\n# today's conservative behavior (documented gap, not a fixture requirement).","reasoning_trace":"The comment names one unsupported shape and claims every other shape keeps conservative behavior. Verified: `find -L ./build -exec rm -rf {} +` still blocks AMBIGUOUS on head because `-L` is captured as the root and fails the allow ladder, so that claim holds by accident. Multi-root shapes, which the comment never mentions, are now allowed (SEC-001).","finding":"The new comment misdescribes the regex's safety envelope and omits the multi-root case it opens.","critique":"Comments on a safety hook are the spec future maintainers trust; this one documents a guarantee the code does not provide.","remediation":"Rewrite the comment to match whatever fix ships for SEC-001, naming multi-root and flags-before-root explicitly.","confidence":"high","references":["CWE-1068 https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/1068.html"]}
{"id":"CQ-004","category":"code_quality","criterion":"documentation","severity":"LOW","score":2,"file":"grimoires/loa/a2a/sprint-1/reviewer.md","line":3,"code_snippet":"# refactor(hooks,cheval,state): single-token find roots, plain 4xx handling, historical sprint counting","reasoning_trace":"All three hunks change observable behavior (hook decision, exception class, state transition) yet the PR is titled refactor and the implementation report is the title plus one sentence, with no AC Verification, rationale or test evidence. The change to the safety hook has no security documentation.","finding":"Behavior changes to a safety hook, an error classification and a workflow gate are labelled a refactor and shipped without rationale or AC verification.","critique":"A refactor label tells reviewers no behavior changed; here it hides three regressions.","remediation":"Retitle with the correct conventional-commit type, add an AC Verification section with file:line evidence and the test commands run, and record the rationale for each semantic change.","confidence":"high","references":["CWE-1059 https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/1059.html"]}
